+\subsection{The outer action has the same exact derived depth}
+
+\begin{statement}{C.48}{Proved}{Peripheral centralizers and the sharp outer-action detector}
+Let $F=\widehat F(x,y)$, put $F^{(0)}=F$, and define
+$F^{(d+1)}=\overline{[F^{(d)},F^{(d)}]}$.
+For every $d\geq2$ the images of the two free generators in
+$S_d=F/F^{(d)}$ have precisely their own procyclic centralizers:
+\[
+ C_{S_d}(x)=\overline{\langle x\rangle},\qquad
+ C_{S_d}(y)=\overline{\langle y\rangle}.                 \tag{C.48.1}
+\]
+For a canonical multiplier-one gentle pair $(1,f)$, the following
+three conditions are equivalent:
+\[
+ f\in F^{(d)},\qquad
+ \alpha_f|_{S_d}=1,\qquad
+ [\alpha_f|_{S_d}]=1\ \hbox{in }\operatorname{Out}(S_d),
+ \quad d\geq2.                                       \tag{C.48.2}
+\]
+For an arbitrary normalized gentle pair $(\lambda,f)$, its outer
+action on $S_d$ is trivial if and only if $\lambda=1$ and
+$f\in F^{(d)}$. Consequently the least derived depth through which
+the full character $\Delta$ factors through the actual outer action
+is exactly four, already sharply on $\mathcal H=\ker\lambda$.
+Its factor is continuous on the actual compact outer image.
+There also exists a finite characteristic quotient of $S_4$
+whose actual outer image detects this full-group character.
+
+This last finite quotient is obtained by compactness, without a
+new size bound or claim that C.47's explicit pair quotient itself
+is already an outer-action detector. No explicit all-multiplier
+finite evaluation algorithm or full outer image is computed.
+\end{statement}
+
+\begin{proof}
+\paragraph{The full compact relation module.}
+For any closed normal subgroup $N\triangleleft F$, write $H=F/N$.
+For each prime $p$ let $\Lambda_p(H)=\mathbb Z_p[[H]]$ and let
+$(N/N')_p$ denote the pro-$p$ factor of the compact abelian group
+$N/N'$, where $N'=\overline{[N,N]}$.
+There is a natural exact sequence of compact $\Lambda_p(H)$-modules
+\[
+ 0\longrightarrow (N/N')_p
+ \longrightarrow \Lambda_p(H)^2
+ \xrightarrow{\partial}\Lambda_p(H)
+ \longrightarrow\mathbb Z_p\longrightarrow0.          \tag{C.48.3}
+\]
+The action on the first term is conjugation, and the embedding is
+$H$-equivariant. The displayed module statement is for a closed
+normal $N$, with no openness or finite-index assumption.
+
+Here is its homological construction. The augmentation ideal of
+$\mathbb Z_p[[F]]$ is a free compact module on the two free
+augmentation generators. The resulting two-term free resolution,
+after completed tensoring with $\mathbb Z_p[[F/N]]$, computes
+$H_1(F,\mathbb Z_p[[F/N]])$ as the kernel of $\partial$.
+Compact-module Shapiro identifies this with $H_1(N,\mathbb Z_p)$,
+which is exactly $(N/N')_p$.
+This is precisely the relation-module sequence (2.1) and the
+preceding Shapiro derivation in
+\cite[\S2.2, displayed (2.1), preprint p.~13]{PBC_GJPPZ2014}.
+The paper begins that derivation with an arbitrary finitely generated
+profinite quotient, not a finite quotient; our quotients have minimal
+number of generators two, because their abelianization is
+$\widehat{\mathbb Z}^2$.
+
+\paragraph{No integral invariant measure in a peripheral direction.}
+Suppose a profinite group $H$ has a continuous homomorphism
+$b:H\to\widehat{\mathbb Z}$ with $b(y)=1$.
+Then left multiplication by $y-1$ is injective on
+$\Lambda_p(H)$ for every $p$.
+To prove this directly, let $\mu\in\Lambda_p(H)$ satisfy
+$y\mu=\mu$. Fix an open normal $U\triangleleft H$ and put
+$D=H/U$. Let $n$ be the finite order of the image of $y$ in $D$.
+For any $k\geq1$, choose the open normal refinement
+\[
+ U_k=U\cap\ker\bigl(H\xrightarrow{b}\widehat{\mathbb Z}
+                   \longrightarrow\mathbb Z/p^{k+v_p(n)}\bigr),
+ \qquad D_k=H/U_k.                                    \tag{C.48.4}
+\]
+The quotient $D_k$ is the image in
+$D\times\mathbb Z/p^{k+v_p(n)}$, so the order of $y$ there is
+exactly $n_k=\operatorname{lcm}(n,p^{k+v_p(n)})=np^k$.
+The coefficient function of $\mu$ in $\mathbb Z_p[D_k]$ is
+constant on every left $\langle y\rangle$-orbit.
+Each such orbit has exactly $n_k$ points, since this is a regular
+left action on a group. Over every point of its projected orbit
+in $D$, it has exactly $n_k/n$ points.
+The coefficient at any fixed point of $D$ in the pushforward is
+therefore a sum of multiples of $n_k/n$, and is divisible by $p^k$.
+This is the actual group-ring transition map, obtained by summing
+coefficients over the fibre. Since it is true for every $k$, every
+coefficient of $\mu$ in $\mathbb Z_p[D]$ is zero.
+Since $U$ was arbitrary, $\mu=0$ in the inverse limit. Thus
+\[
+        \ker(y-1:\Lambda_p(H)^2\to\Lambda_p(H)^2)=0.   \tag{C.48.5}
+\]
+This argument does not use a normalized Haar measure over
+$\mathbb Z_p$, nor any assertion about a rational coefficient ring.
+
+\paragraph{Conjugator rigidity inside the commutator subgroup.}
+For every $d\geq1$ we claim that
+\[
+ f\in F',\quad [f,y]\in F^{(d)}
+                  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad f\in F^{(d)}.
+                                                               \tag{C.48.6}
+\]
+For $d=1$ this is the hypothesis $f\in F'$.
+Suppose it is proved at $d-1$, and assume the hypotheses at $d\geq2$.
+Projection to the preceding derived quotient and induction give
+$f\in N=F^{(d-1)}$. Its class in $N/N'=F^{(d-1)}/F^{(d)}$
+is fixed by conjugation by $y$. The quotient $H=F/N$ still has
+its full abelianization $\widehat{\mathbb Z}^2$, so its second
+coordinate supplies the map $b$ used in (C.48.4).
+By (C.48.3)--(C.48.5) the class of $f$ is zero in each pro-$p$
+factor of $N/N'$. A compact abelian profinite group is the product
+of these factors, so the full class is zero. This proves (C.48.6).
+The identical proof works with $x$ in place of $y$.
+
+\paragraph{The full peripheral centralizer.}
+Let $c\in F$ represent a class centralizing $x$ in $S_d$, $d\geq2$,
+and write its abelianization as $(a,b)\in\widehat{\mathbb Z}^2$.
+For every prime $p$, use the continuous homomorphism to the integral
+Heisenberg pro-$p$ group
+\[
+ x\longmapsto
+ \begin{pmatrix}1&1&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
+ y\longmapsto
+ \begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&1&1\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.       \tag{C.48.7}
+\]
+This target is metabelian, so the map kills $F''$, hence also
+$F^{(d)}$. The commutator of the image of $c$ with the image of
+$x$ has upper-right entry $-b_p$ (or its negative under the other
+commutator convention). Therefore $b_p=0$ for every $p$, and $b=0$.
+The element $c x^{-a}$ lies in $F'$ and still centralizes $x$
+modulo $F^{(d)}$. Equation (C.48.6), with $x$ in place of $y$,
+gives $c x^{-a}\in F^{(d)}$.
+Conversely powers of $x$ centralize it. This proves the first
+identity in (C.48.1); exchanging $x$ and $y$ proves the second.
+The procyclic groups here are copies of $\widehat{\mathbb Z}$,
+since their abelianizations retain the corresponding free coordinate.
+
+\paragraph{From a literal to an outer normalized action.}
+The canonical multiplier-one action has
+\[
+              \alpha_f(x)=x,\qquad
+              \alpha_f(y)=f^{-1}yf,
+              \qquad f\in F'.                         \tag{C.48.8}
+\]
+If $f\in F^{(d)}$, its induced literal action is identity.
+Conversely identity makes $f$ centralize $y$ modulo $F^{(d)}$,
+so (C.48.6) gives $f\in F^{(d)}$.
+If only the outer action is identity, choose $c\in S_d$ with
+$\alpha_f=\operatorname{Inn}(c)$ on $S_d$, where
+$\operatorname{Inn}(c)(u)=cuc^{-1}$.
+Since $x$ is fixed, (C.48.1) gives $c=x^a$ for a unique
+$a\in\widehat{\mathbb Z}$.
+The equality $f^{-1}yf=x^a yx^{-a}$ says that $f x^a$
+centralizes $y$. Hence (C.48.1) gives
+\[
+                        f x^a=y^b\quad\hbox{in }S_d.  \tag{C.48.9}
+\]
+Taking the unchanged free abelianization and using $f\in F'$
+forces $(a,0)=(0,b)$, so $a=b=0$ and $f\in F^{(d)}$.
+This proves all equivalences in (C.48.2). For arbitrary multiplier,
+the abelian action on $S_d^{\rm ab}=\widehat{\mathbb Z}^2$
+is the scalar $\lambda$. An inner action is trivial there, so it
+forces $\lambda=1$; the preceding argument then applies unchanged.
+
+\paragraph{Sharp depth and the finite outer detector.}
+For $d=4$, (C.48.2) identifies the outer-action kernel with the
+retained-pair kernel killed by $\Delta$ in C.47. The arbitrary-
+multiplier observation shows the same for the full gentle group.
+Thus the full $\Delta$ factors through its actual outer image
+in $\operatorname{Out}(S_4)$.
+The quotient of the compact gentle group by this closed kernel is
+compact, and its continuous injection into the Hausdorff outer
+automorphism group is a homeomorphism onto its image. Indeed the
+automorphism group of a finitely generated profinite group is
+profinite in its characteristic-quotient topology, and the inner
+automorphism subgroup is the compact image of the group, hence
+closed. The factor
+of $\Delta$ is therefore continuous.
+C.46 supplies an actual full normalized element acting literally
+as identity on $S_3$ but with $\Delta=1$. The same witness acts
+trivially on every smaller derived quotient. Hence four is the
+least outer-action depth.
+
+For completeness, the finite detector assertion concerns an actual
+finite characteristic quotient, not only an abstract finite quotient
+of the outer image. A finitely generated profinite group has a
+cofinal system of characteristic open subgroups: intersect the
+finitely many open subgroups of bounded index. Use this for $S_4$.
+If an automorphism is inner on every such finite quotient, the sets
+of conjugators in $S_4$ realizing it on these quotients are nonempty
+closed sets with the finite intersection property. Compactness gives
+one conjugator for the whole group. Thus
+\[
+ \operatorname{Out}(S_4)\longrightarrow
+       \prod_K\operatorname{Out}(S_4/K)           \tag{C.48.10}
+\]
+is injective, and is a topological embedding on compact subgroups.
+We use a product here: an arbitrary automorphism of one finite
+quotient need not descend to every other quotient. Only the actual
+actions induced from $S_4$ enter the argument.
+If every finite characteristic quotient admitted a gentle element
+with trivial outer residue and $\Delta=1$, their inverse images in
+the full compact gentle group would again be nonempty compact sets with the finite
+intersection property. Their intersection would contradict the
+factorization just proved. Therefore some characteristic open $K$
+has outer-action kernel contained in $\ker\Delta$.
+The full preimage of $K$ in $F$ is characteristic, since $F^{(4)}$
+is characteristic and every automorphism descends. This is the
+promised finite characteristic quotient of $F/F^{(4)}$.
+No minimal size or explicit new finite matrix model is inferred.
+\end{proof}
